To add a link to every post on your website, you'll need to modify the Nginx configuration and add a new file to handle the posts.

First, let's create a new file called `posts.conf` and add the following content:
```nginx
location ~ ^(.*)\.html$ {
    if ($request_uri ~* "/posts/(.*)") {
        set $post_id $1;
        include posts/header.html;
    }
    include /usr/share/nginx/html$uri;
}
```
This code will catch any incoming requests for HTML files and check if the URI contains the string "/posts/". If it does, it will extract the post ID from the URI and include a custom header.html file to generate the post link.

Next, let's create a new file called `header.html` and add the following content:
```html
<a href="/posts/{{ post_id }}">{{ post_id }}</a>
```
This code will include the post ID in the anchor tag and create a link to the post.

Finally, let's modify the original configuration file to include the new posts.conf file:
```nginx
server {
    # ...

    location / {
        root /usr/share/nginx/html;
        index index.html index.htm;
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;

        # Include the posts.conf file
        include posts.conf;
    }

    # ...
}
```
With these changes, every HTML file on your website will now include a link to the corresponding post. If the post ID is in the URI, it will use the custom header.html file to generate the link.

Remember to update the posts.conf file to include the necessary templates and logic to handle your specific post structure and formatting.

